   712	
   713	[2026-08-13; GATE 1 of the avenues sweep xmodel/sol-avenues2.md §1.
   714	Adversarial re-derivation in THIS document's chart conventions.
   715	VERDICT: THE LEMMA IS VALID HERE — 9 pins, 6 live in the banked
   716	residual-32 system, each a PURE VARIABLE pin. Derivation:]
   717	
   718	**(1) Closed, hence exact, on A^2.** J(f,g) = c != 0 constant gives
   719	d(f dg - c x dy) = (J - c) dx^dy = 0 and d(g df - c y dx) = 0.
   720	H^1_dR(A^2) = 0 in char 0 (elementary: integrate the closed form
   721	term-by-term in x, the remainder is a closed form in y alone), so
   722	there are POLYNOMIALS P, Q with f dg - c x dy = dP and
   723	g df - c y dx = dQ. Our banked gauge has c = 1 (S_R = G_R = 1);
   724	nothing below consumes the value beyond c != 0.
   725	
   726	**(2) Pull back along a fibre branch.** §2b (promoted, F1):
   727	f - a = phi_f(x) prod_i (y - y_i(x)), g = phi_g(x) prod_j (y - z_j(x))
   728	— the y_i are EXACT root series of f = a, the z_j of g = 0. Let
   729	phi: Spec K((t)) -> A^2, phi = (x(t), y_i(t)) be an f-place. Then
   730	phi*f = a IDENTICALLY, so pulling back g df - y dx = dQ kills the
   731	first term: y_i(t) dx(t) = -d(Q o phi). Q is a polynomial and x, y_i
   732	are Laurent, so Q o phi in K((t)) — finite principal part, NO log,
   733	NO essential part (this is the entire "no-log" content; a log t in a
   734	primitive is exactly what a nonzero residue would require). Purely
   735	formal; no convergence is consumed. Hence Res_t(y_i dx) = 0 at EVERY
   736	f-place separately (the primitive is global-polynomial, so this is
   737	per-place, strictly stronger than the residue-theorem sum). On g = 0
   738	the same argument with f dg - x dy = dP (phi*g = 0, so the f dg term
   739	dies) gives Res_t(x(t) dz_j(t)) = 0 at every g-place.
